# deploy/herald-watch.service
#
# systemd --user unit for one `pnpm watch` tick — collect, and (only if collect found anything new)
# translate:prepare + claude -p, then translate:align + claude -p. See
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-05-watch-scheduler-design.md for the full design.
#
# NOT installed by any committed script. Copying this into ~/.config/systemd/user/ and running
# `systemctl --user daemon-reload` is a later, human-supervised step (see the team runbook).
#
# Machine-specific by design, not templated: the absolute paths below were found with
# `command -v pnpm|node|claude` on the one machine this runs on (2026-08-05, kyle@this box). A
# different machine needs its own copy of this file with its own paths, not a parameterised one.
# The exception is node and pnpm, which are reached through `%h/.herald/bin` — see the PATH= comment
# below for why those two stopped being written out directly on 2026-08-09.

[Unit]
Description=Mantle KR Herald watch scheduler — one tick (collect -> translate:prepare -> translate:align)
# `OnFailure=` can only name a unit, never a bare script, so the actual notification logic lives in
# deploy/herald-notify-failure.sh, run via the templated wrapper unit
# deploy/herald-notify-failure@.service names. `%n` below expands to this unit's own full name
# (herald-watch.service) before systemd resolves the OnFailure= target — the exact
# `OnFailure=bar@%n.service` idiom documented in `man systemd.unit`'s own OnFailure= example — so
# that name becomes the templated unit's `%i`, and this unit is always the one named and tailed on
# its own failure, never confused with herald-x-reconcile.service's (see
# deploy/herald-notify-failure@.service's own header for why the hook had to become templated at
# all). Fires on ANY non-zero exit from ExecStart, including this unit's own TimeoutStartSec= below
# — a silently dead scheduler is the one failure this whole feature exists to prevent.
OnFailure=herald-notify-failure@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# The deploy checkout — merged `main` only, never the tree anyone edits. A user unit runs
# `pnpm <script>` out of this directory, so whatever is checked out here IS what production
# runs the next time the timer fires. While this pointed at the development checkout, every
# feature branch was live in production the moment it was checked out: on 2026-08-07 a branch
# adding `translations.published_text` made the 18:41 fire query a column production did not
# have, which exited non-zero and fired the OnFailure= Telegram hook below. Nothing was wrong
# with that branch — it simply was not merged yet.
#
# Updated only by deploy/herald-deploy.sh (fetch, hard reset to origin/main, install, copy in
# the git-ignored steering config, migrate). That config was symlinked into the development
# checkout until 2026-08-09; it is a deploy-time copy now, so editing the development .env or
# glossary no longer reaches this directory until someone deploys.
# tests/deploy/workingDirectory.test.ts fails if this line ever names a development checkout again.
WorkingDirectory=%h/.herald/app

# pnpm, node, and claude — none of them are on a systemd user unit's default PATH. node must be
# reachable even though pnpm is invoked below by its absolute path: pnpm's own file starts with
# `#!/usr/bin/env node`, and `env` resolves "node" via this PATH, not via ExecStart='s path.
# claude is spawned from inside the Node process by ClaudeCodeAgent (src/adapters/agent/).
#
# `%h/.herald/bin` is a directory of symlinks (node, pnpm), NOT an nvm version directory. An nvm path
# carries its version — `.../v24.14.0/bin` — and nvm deletes that directory when you upgrade, so a
# unit naming one dies at the next fire with 203/EXEC before it runs a single line. The timer keeps
# firing, every fire fails identically, and the only signal is the OnFailure= alert. A 2026-08-09
# rebuild of this machine produced exactly that: the box came back with v24.19.0 while all three
# units still named v24.14.0. After a node upgrade, re-point the symlinks and leave these units
# alone:
#   mkdir -p ~/.herald/bin
#   ln -sfn "$(which node)" ~/.herald/bin/node
#   ln -sfn "$(which pnpm)" ~/.herald/bin/pnpm
Environment=PATH=%h/.herald/bin:/home/kyle/.local/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# The scheduler's own artifact tree — never the repo's own output/. This is what keeps the
# file-backed collect watermark (output/x/state.json; see src/cli/stores.ts's doc comment on why it
# stayed file-backed when everything else moved to Postgres) from being shared with a hand-run
# local `pnpm collect`. During this plan's own implementation, a dev-database run advanced that one
# shared file past 39 threads a production run would then have skipped forever — see src/paths.ts's
# OUTPUT_DIR doc comment for the full incident and why the override is resolved to an absolute path.
Environment=HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR=%h/.herald/output

# The oldest post this scheduler will ever translate, handed to `translate:prepare --since`.
# Without it, `PrepareTranslations` takes the first `--limit` of the *whole* untranslated set,
# oldest first: measured against production on 2026-08-06, 211 items reaching back to 2026-06-01,
# so 3 items every two hours would have spent roughly six days on history before reaching the 23
# threads that same first tick had just collected.
#
# The value is not arbitrary. It sat at 2026-07-27T14:35:24Z — the creation time of
# x:2081748977918337053, the newest source post the Korean account had translated before this
# scheduler existed, since the team's standing rule is that the backlog cutoff is the last
# translated post. It is one second later now, and that second is the whole point: `applySelector`
# compares `item.createdAt >= since` (src/app/PrepareTranslations.ts), so a floor set *at* a post's
# own timestamp still selects that post. On 2026-08-06 Kyle dropped that item's translation as
# stale — the Korean account had already covered the ground by hand — and with the floor on the
# boundary the next tick would have spent an agent turn re-translating it straight back into the
# review queue. Moving to :25 excludes exactly that one post and nothing else.
#
# It is still the same instant %h/.herald/output/x/state.json was seeded with, to the second, so the
# collect floor and the translate floor remain one decision rather than two that can drift apart —
# tests/deploy/watchCutoff.test.ts holds them equal.
#
# It gates THIS ACCOUNT's posts only. `applySelector` exempts an item whose author is known and is
# not the account `pnpm collect` sweeps (src/domain/sweptAccount.ts), because nothing but the
# dashboard's 링크 수집 tab can put another account's thread in `x_threads` and one hand-picked link
# is not the backlog this floor exists to hold back. A pre-floor post from THIS account is refused
# when someone tries to link it, rather than collected and then never translated.
#
# Raising it further skips content permanently; lowering it re-opens backlog. Either is a content
# decision, not a deployment one.
Environment=HERALD_TRANSLATE_SINCE=2026-07-27T14:35:25.000Z

# The tick's throughput dial: how many items each of translate:prepare/translate:align gets via
# --limit. Deliberately shipped commented out rather than set, because the code's default is the
# same 3 this scheduler was armed with (DEFAULT_WATCH_BATCH, src/cli/watchBatch.ts) and a duplicated
# value is one more thing to drift. Uncomment it to tune throughput without a deploy: at this
# timer's two-hour cadence the pipeline moves batch * 24 / 2 items per day, and whether that keeps
# pace with collection is read off the backlog, not off the code. A value that is not a positive
# integer refuses the tick at startup (see src/cli/watch.ts) instead of reaching `--limit` as
# garbage, and every tick's first journal line prints the value it actually ran with. Read the
# TimeoutStartSec= note below before raising it — the bound that matters is per `claude -p` call.
#Environment=HERALD_WATCH_BATCH=3

# Exactly two lines — DATABASE_URL and HERALD_DB_ENV=production — chmod 600, created by Kyle by
# hand (a production connection string does not pass through an agent session). Everything else
# (TWITTERAPI_IO_KEY, TELEGRAM_*, ...) still comes from the repo's own .env via `pnpm watch`'s own
# `tsx --env-file-if-exists=.env`: a shell-exported variable wins over Node's --env-file (verified
# on this machine), so these two override .env's local-Docker DATABASE_URL without this unit ever
# touching .env itself.
EnvironmentFile=%h/.herald/prod.env

# Outer bound on a wedged run. ClaudeCodeAgent already times out at 10 minutes per `claude -p` call
# (src/adapters/agent/ClaudeCodeAgent.ts), and one tick makes at most two of those (translation,
# then alignment): 2 x 600s = 1200s. collect / translate:prepare / status (twice, bracketing the
# translation pass — see WatchTick) / translate:align are ordinary network+DB calls that normally
# finish in seconds, but together get a generous 600s of their own on top
# rather than trimming the agent headroom: 1200s + 600s = 1800s (30 minutes) total. Without this,
# a wedged `claude -p` would leave the unit "active" forever, and systemd skips every later timer
# fire while a unit is still active — a scheduler that looks armed but has silently stopped. The
# upper bound matters just as much as the lower one: 1800s must stay well under herald-watch.timer's
# 7200s (two-hour) period, or a single wedged tick would still be "active" when the next OnCalendar=
# fire comes due, and that fire is the one systemd skips — so the margin here (1800s used out of a
# 7200s budget, a 4x cushion) is deliberate, not incidental. Raising HERALD_WATCH_BATCH does not
# require raising this bound either: ClaudeCodeAgent is invoked once per worksheet, and one tick
# makes at most two such calls (translation, then alignment) regardless of how many items each
# worksheet holds, so the 2 x 600s + 600s arithmetic above is unchanged by a bigger batch.
# What *does* bound a sane batch size is the other timeout, the one inside each of those calls:
# ClaudeCodeAgent's own DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS caps a single `claude -p` at 600s, and a worksheet of 30
# items takes longer inside that one call than a worksheet of 3 — so the batch's real ceiling is
# per-call, not this unit's total, and "this number doesn't have to change" is not the same as
# "there is nothing to check". Raise the batch in steps and read the tick's duration in the journal.
TimeoutStartSec=1800

# Wrapped in deploy/herald-run-logged.sh rather than invoked directly, so that every tick leaves a
# durable run log under %h/.herald/logs/herald-watch/ as well as in the journal. The journal alone
# is not a record on this box: journald rotates on every backwards clock step, this machine's WSL2
# host sync and timesyncd both step the clock constantly, and the readable window has been measured
# at roughly eight minutes — a successful 02:17 tick had zero recoverable lines by 03:30 the same
# morning. That leaves "the tick succeeded but did the wrong thing" with no evidence at all, and
# TimeoutStartSec= above is a bound whose only diagnosis is the log of the run it killed.
#
# `%n` is this unit's own name, and it keys the per-unit log directory — the same key
# deploy/herald-notify-failure.sh derives its fallback excerpt from, so the two agree without either
# hardcoding a unit list. The wrapper's own header explains why `StandardOutput=append:` is not used
# instead (measured: it replaces the journal rather than adding to it, which would break the
# OnFailure= hook's excerpt), and why the tick's exit status still reaches systemd unchanged —
# a `| tee` that swallowed a non-zero exit would silently switch this unit's Telegram alert off
# forever. tests/deploy/runLogging.test.ts is what holds that.
#
# Ordering note for whoever installs this: the wrapper lives in the deploy checkout, so
# `bash deploy/herald-deploy.sh` must have run (landing this commit in %h/.herald/app) BEFORE this
# unit file is copied into ~/.config/systemd/user/ — otherwise the next fire runs an ExecStart= that
# does not exist yet.
ExecStart=%h/.herald/app/deploy/herald-run-logged.sh %n %h/.herald/bin/pnpm watch
